# Day 6 - EC2 Instance Connect, Security Groups and EC2 OS Basics

## Topics Learned

- Connected to EC2 instances using AWS Console EC2 Instance Connect.
- Understood that EC2 Instance Connect provides browser-based SSH access to an EC2 instance.
- Understood that SSH uses TCP port 22.
- Configured the Security Group to allow SSH access through the AWS EC2 Instance Connect regional prefix list.
- Understood the difference between browser-based EC2 Instance Connect and normal SSH from a local terminal.
- Connected to two EC2 instances separately and understood that each instance has its own terminal session and network information.
- Observed that different EC2 instances can use different operating systems/AMIs.
- Understood the difference between Ubuntu and `ec2-user`.
- Ubuntu is an operating system, while `ubuntu` is commonly its default login username.
- `ec2-user` is a common default login username for Amazon Linux.
- Understood that the default login username depends on the operating system/AMI.
- Connected to an Ubuntu 26.04 LTS EC2 instance and successfully ran:
  `sudo apt update`
- Used `exit` to close the EC2 Instance Connect session.
- Observed that different EC2 instances can have different private and public IP addresses.
- Understood that EC2 instances must be accessed independently even when both are part of the same AWS environment.

## Practical Learning

EC2 Instance Connect flow:

Browser → AWS Console → EC2 Instance Connect → SSH/TCP 22 → EC2 Terminal

Basic OS/login relationship:

Ubuntu OS → `ubuntu` user → `apt`

Amazon Linux → `ec2-user` user → `dnf`/`yum`

## Important Troubleshooting Understanding

- EC2 Instance Connect is different from normal SSH from a local terminal.
- Browser-based EC2 Instance Connect requires the appropriate Security Group SSH rule.
- EC2 health checks passing does not automatically mean SSH access is working.
- The correct login username depends on the instance operating system/AMI.
- Different EC2 instances can have different operating systems, usernames, private IPs and public IPs.
